TA-sensors (Linux hwmon Sensors Telemetry Add-on)
==================================================

Scripted-input add-on that collects Linux hwmon telemetry (the lm-sensors
data model) directly from /sys/class/hwmon - designed for BMC-less hosts
where IPMI/ipmitool is unavailable: consumer desktops, gaming rigs, VMs,
SBCs and any machine without a management controller.

No third-party dependencies: stdlib-only python3. No ipmitool, no lm-sensors
(sensors(1)), no kernel modules beyond the hwmon drivers your kernel already
loads (coretemp, k10temp, nvme, acpitz, r8169, mt7921, ...).

Prerequisites
-------------
  * Linux kernel with hwmon sensors registered under /sys/class/hwmon
    (check: ls /sys/class/hwmon/*/name). If that list is empty there is
    nothing for this add-on to collect.
  * Splunk Enterprise or Universal Forwarder 9.x - 10.6 (forwarder is the
    recommended deployment on monitored hosts).
  * python3.9+ available to Splunk (all Splunk 9.x/10.x builds ship one;
    inputs request python.version = python3).
  * A metrics-typed index (defaults to "metrics"): the flat sensor stream
    is emitted as real metric events via METRIC-SCHEMA, so the destination
    index must have datatype = metric, e.g. in indexes.conf:

      [metrics]
      datatype = metric
      homePath = $SPLUNK_DB/metrics/db
      coldPath = $SPLUNK_DB/metrics/colddb
      thawedPath = $SPLUNK_DB/metrics/thaweddb

    On forwarders the index only needs to exist on the indexer.
  * /sys/class/hwmon must be world-readable (it is by default).
  * No network egress, no root, and no additional OS packages required.
  * No dependent apps. TA-sensors is fully standalone; it pairs nicely
    with TA-IPMI (which covers BMC-equipped servers) but does not require
    it.

Install
-------
Forwarder hosts (recommended): install on each monitored Linux host, or
push via deployment server. Indexer side: install on the search head for
the dashboards, and make sure props.conf/transforms.conf ship to the
indexer (app deployment keeps these together).

  1. Install TA-sensors (Splunk UI > Apps > Install app from file, or
     unpack into $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/apps/ on each host).
  2. Restart Splunk.
  3. Confirm data: index=main sourcetype="sensors:status" | head

On forwarder-only deployments the two scripted inputs run on the
forwarder; index-time rules (metric schema) apply on the indexer.

What is collected
-----------------
Two scripted inputs (default interval 60s, both enabled):

  sensors_events.py   index=main     sourcetype=sensors:status
      One nested-JSON event per collection cycle:
      {"chips": {"<chip>": {"<key>": {"sensor_name","reading",
      "reading_value","units","status"}}}}
      Chip and key names are sanitized to [a-z0-9_]; duplicate chip names
      (e.g. several "nvme" drives) get their hwmon number appended so
      every drive stays visible.

  sensors_metrics.py  index=metrics  sourcetype=sensors:metrics
      One metric event per cycle; each numeric sensor becomes a metric
      named  sensors.<chip>_<key>  plus a  <metric>_status  string
      ("ok" or "alarm"). Metric names pass mstats/mcatalog filters.

Families: temp (celsius), in (volts), power (watts), current (amps),
humidity (percent_rh), fan (rpm). Milli-scaled sysfs inputs are
normalized by /1000 (fan stays raw rpm). Sensors whose "_alarm" latch is
set report status "alarm"; "crit"/"max" limits are collected as-is in the
events stream if present.

Dashboards
----------
  Sensors Overview   hosts reporting, current readings (mstats), and an
                     "not ok" / alarm alarm table straight off the
                     events stream.
  Sensors Metrics    mstats time-series panels: CPU cores, temperatures
                     (incl. NVMe/pack- age), fans, voltages, power draw,
                     and live sensors.* metric count.
                     Requires the metrics-typed index above.

Verify / search quick reference
-------------------------------
  index=main sourcetype="sensors:status" | head
  | mcatalog values(metric_name) where index=metrics metric_name="sensors.*" by host
  | mstats avg("sensors.*") where index=metrics span=60s by host

Configure
---------
Override interval or destination index in
etc/apps/TA-sensors/local/inputs.conf (never edit default/). To run on a
subset of hosts, deploy the app per-host or disable unneeded inputs with
disabled = 1. To emit only events (no metrics), disable sensors_metrics.py.

Troubleshooting
---------------
  * No data: check ls /sys/class/hwmon/*/name on the host, then
    "$SPLUNK_HOME/bin/splunk cmd python <app>/bin/sensors_metrics.py"
    from the host - it prints the exact JSON Splunk will ingest.
  * Status events but no metrics: the "metrics" index is missing or is
    not datatype = metric - metric events to an event-type index (and
    plain events to a metric index) are silently dropped.
  * Metric dashboards blank: metric searches must use mstats/mcatalog;
    plain "search index=metrics" returns nothing by design on a
    metric-typed index.
  * Wildcard metric filters: use a trailing wildcard (avg("sensors.*"))
    or a | search metric_name="*cpu*" filter; mid-string wildcards in
    mstats series arguments are not supported.

License
-------
SOURCE-AVAILABLE (see LICENSE) - free personal, educational and evaluation
use; commercial or production use requires a paid license from BentleyPC
LLC (https://www.bentleypc.com/TA-sensors).

Enter your key (format BP-...) after purchase using EITHER method:

  1. Splunk Web: open the app, click "License" in the nav, click a row to
     open the key entry page, paste the key and Save.

  2. File (headless / forwarder friendly): create the file
     $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/apps/TA-sensors/local/license.conf with:

         [settings]
         telemetry_enabled = 1
         key = BP-xxxxxxxxxxxx

     No restart required - the hourly beacon reads this file on every run.

Set telemetry_enabled = 0 to keep the key for validation but pause all
outbound beacons. The beacon sends only: license key, hostname, app name
and app version to the BentleyPC license API (see api_base in
default/license.conf). It is telemetry only - never a license check that
blocks functionality.
